<p class="lead">For much of the year in Southeast Texas, the best time to be outside is after the sun goes down. Patios, outdoor kitchens, pergolas and pools are where many families spend their evenings, yet they are often lit by a single bright fixture by the back door. Spring Landscape Lighting designs patio lighting in Spring, TX that makes these outdoor rooms comfortable: enough light to cook, move and see faces, without glare, without bugs swarming a bare bulb, and without turning the backyard into a stage.</p>

<h2>What's included</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Task lighting for grills, outdoor kitchens, bars and serving counters.</li>
  <li>Ambient light for seating and dining areas, under covered patios, pergolas and cabanas.</li>
  <li>Step, deck and hardscape lighting at level changes, pool coping transitions and seat walls.</li>
  <li>Landscape lighting around the pool: trees, planting beds, water features and the view from the house.</li>
  <li>Accent lighting for fire pits, fireplaces, outdoor TVs and other features, plus zoning and dimming so each area can be set independently.</li>
</ul>
<p>Underwater pool lights are usually part of the pool equipment and handled by your pool builder or electrician. We design the landscape and hardscape lighting around them so the two systems look like one plan.</p>

<h2>Fixtures and techniques</h2>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Hardscape lights</h3><p>Low-profile LED fixtures tucked under seat-wall caps, counter edges and step treads. The light source stays hidden and the surface glows.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Downlights</h3><p>Small fixtures in pergola beams, patio ceilings and trees that light the table and floor rather than people's eyes.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Accent uplights</h3><p>Light on the trees and plantings around the space so the edges of the yard do not feel like a black wall.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Dimmable zones</h3><p>Separate control for the kitchen, dining and pool areas so you can go from cleanup brightness to a quiet evening level.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Outdoor living lighting relies on low, layered output. Hardscape fixtures typically draw 2 to 5 watts each. Kitchen task lights may need more, and we place them so the cook is not standing in their own shadow. Color temperature stays warm, usually 2700K, because skin tones, food and wood finishes look better in warm light and it feels more relaxed. Our <a href="/warm-white-vs-cool-white-landscape-lighting/">warm white vs. cool white guide</a> explains why.</p>

<h2>Designing for Spring, TX backyards</h2>
<h3>Heat, humidity and insects</h3>
<p>Summer evenings here are warm and humid, and bright, exposed light sources attract insects. Lower-output, shielded fixtures and warm color temperatures tend to draw fewer bugs to the seating area than a single bright floodlight. We also keep fixtures away from spots where they would heat up surfaces people touch.</p>
<h3>Pools, water and chemicals</h3>
<p>Fixtures near a pool face splash-out, chlorine or salt, and constant moisture. We specify corrosion-resistant fixtures, keep wire connections out of splash zones, and follow the clearance rules that apply to electrical equipment near pools. The low-voltage transformer is mounted at a safe distance with a proper GFCI-protected supply.</p>
<h3>Covered patios and pergolas</h3>
<p>Many Spring homes have covered patios with fans and a single ceiling fixture. Adding small downlights in beams or using wall-mounted fixtures gives a more even, flattering light. Wiring routes are planned around the structure so they are hidden, not stapled along beams.</p>
<h3>Views from inside</h3>
<p>Large back windows turn into mirrors at night when the yard is dark. Even modest lighting on trees and beds beyond the patio lets you see out, and makes the yard feel like part of the house.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Think about the evening in stages: cooking, dinner, after dinner. Each stage wants a different level of light, which is why dimming and zoning matter more outdoors than most people expect.</div>

<h2>Common mistakes we avoid</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One bright floodlight.</strong> It lights the patio and blinds everyone sitting on it. Layered low light is more comfortable and more useful.</li>
  <li><strong>Visible light sources at eye level.</strong> Seated eye level is lower than standing, so fixtures that seem fine from the door can glare at guests. We check from the chairs.</li>
  <li><strong>Lighting the pool area but nothing beyond.</strong> Without light on the surrounding landscape, the yard ends abruptly in darkness.</li>
  <li><strong>Glare across the water.</strong> Light reflected off the pool surface can shine directly back at the house. Aiming and fixture position matter.</li>
  <li><strong>No separate control.</strong> If the patio lights are on the same circuit as everything else, they are either always on or always off. Zones solve this.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote class="pull">An outdoor room should feel lit like a room, not like a parking lot.</blockquote>

<h2>Connecting the backyard to the rest of the design</h2>
<p>Patio and pool spaces rarely stand alone. Mature trees around the yard often deserve <a href="/services/tree-garden-lighting/">tree and garden lighting</a>, side paths need <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway lighting</a> to connect front to back, and <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a> let you set scenes for entertaining and quiet nights from your phone. If you are curious what an outdoor living system costs to operate, the <a href="/tools/energy-cost-calculator/">energy cost calculator</a> will give you a quick estimate, and our article on <a href="/electricity-landscape-lighting-use/">how much electricity landscape lighting uses</a> walks through the math. Spring Landscape Lighting will also review your backyard at night so the plan reflects how you really use it.</p>

